│   ├── mvnw
│   └── mvnw.cmd
│
├── frontend/
│   ├── public/
│   ├── src/
│   ├── package.json
│   ├── tsconfig.json
│   └── yarn.lock
│
└── README.md
```

## ⚙️ Backend

The backend is implemented using Spring Boot and provides the application's REST services.

Main technologies include:

- Spring Web
- Spring Data JPA
- Spring Security
- H2 for local development
- PostgreSQL support

### Running the Backend

Navigate to the backend directory:

```bash
cd backend
```

On Linux/macOS:

```bash
./mvnw spring-boot:run
```

On Windows:

```bash
mvnw.cmd spring-boot:run
```

## 🖥 Frontend

The frontend was built with React and TypeScript.

Navigate to:

```bash
cd frontend
```

Install the dependencies:

```bash
yarn install
```

Start the application:

```bash
yarn start
```

## 🧪 Frontend Tests

The frontend includes support for automated tests using Jest and React Testing Library.

Run the tests with:

```bash
yarn test
```

## 🗄 Database

The backend supports:

- H2 Database
- PostgreSQL

H2 can be used for local development and testing, while PostgreSQL can be used as a persistent database environment.

## 🧠 What This Project Demonstrates

This project helped me understand how the different layers of a web application work together:

- React frontend
- REST API
- Spring Boot backend
- Database persistence
- HTTP communication
- Data modeling
- Application testing

From a QA perspective, understanding the complete application architecture also helps when designing better integration and end-to-end test strategies.

## 📚 Learning Context

This repository was developed during **Spring React Week by DevSuperior**.

The project represents part of my studies in full stack development and helped expand my understanding beyond test automation into the architecture and implementation of the systems being tested.

## 📌 Project Status

This is a **study and reference project**.

The framework and dependency versions reflect the period when the project was developed and may require updates to run in a modern environment.
